neous  equations just by crunching the algebra  right by doing some substitution  bit of rearrangement and then your  solutions um arise from these equations  but what this question is asking us to  do is to think about them graphically  you can see it says  for the moment assume that both a and b  are positive then sketch the graphs  of these two equations on the same  number plane  now we we haven't been sketching graphs  all that recently  on a cartesian plane certainly but uh we  need to call back all that knowledge and  skill to be able to handle  these two guys here okay so what we're  going to have a look at is trying to  understand the graphs of these  on the same number plane and see what  conclusions we can draw from this okay  so the first one which we got from the  real components of the left hand right  hand side  is x squared minus y squared equals a  we're going to  see what that looks like so if you go to  some graphing software  you'll be able to put together a graph  of this and i'm appealing to this right  away  because in the old version of the  extension 2 course  there was a topic called conics which  was short for conic  sections conic as it comes from a cone  because there's a wonderful series of  shapes which seem all disconnected  that we've learned about in mathematics  already circles  ellipses parabolas and the shape you're  about to see they're all connected  by their relationship to the cone now  let me try and explain what's going on  i'm going to graph the equation that  they suggested to us before  x squared and then i think they said  minus y squared and that's going to be  equal to  a now you can see i've got a set to  equal one for  uh for a moment just before i type in  one i want you to have a think maybe  pause the video  and um try and contemplate this if  you've never seen an equation like this  before  what shape would you predict to emerge  from it  um you know you know what y equals x  squared looks like that's a parabola  you know what x squared plus y squared  equals a constant you know what that is  that's a that's a circle right  so what happens when you get x squared  minus  y squared what kind of shape would  emerge from that  like i said i hope you spend some time  to have to think about before  you now play and see what's going to go  on when i put in it's equal to  a watch what shape emerges  what is this uh well it's very strange  shape for starters  it's not a function if you think back to  earlier uh in year 11  when we're looking at functions we said  that if you put in one input and x value  you should get one  output one y value if i put in something  like x  equals two in fact i'll just quickly  draw x equals two x equals two  there's a single x value and it does not  have just one y value one output it's  got two of them  looks like plus or minus a square root  of three okay um so  this is not a function it is what we  call what we classify a relation  um but what other kind of way do you  have to describe this shape let's get  rid of this we don't need anymore  this actually is a shape that's  surprisingly familiar you just haven't  looked at it in this orientation  before so if i just go back to where i  was drawing  here's one i prepared earlier if you  have a look at  oh no i'm partially deleting that's not  what i want to do if you have a look at  this shape  here oh go away i'm highlighting why am  i highlighting there we go  there's my highlighting by accident  there's the shape which we just got out  of uh  doing this graph what i'm going to do is  something a bit unusual i'm going to  take this shape  and i'm going to rotate it and i hope  that once i've done the rotation it will  look a little more familiar  so let me spin this around and i'm going  to spin it exactly  45 degrees that looks  pretty alright to me let's move that  down the page a little bit  if it's still not looking obvious let me  put in  an extra line or two i'm going to put  one  vertically through let's get this  straight there we go  vertically through there and i'm going  to match it with one horizontally  through here ah that's pretty close  i'm reasonably satisfied with that all  right when you have a look at this  does this look like a familiar shape  this is of course  the hyperbola even though from the  original you saw  um we had to rotate this thing through  45 degrees or pi on four  radians so this is the same shape but  it's looked at at a different  orientation this is still a hyperbola so  it's just a different kind of hyperbola  than you've dealt with like i said  it used to be dealt with in the old  course so you can have a look at there  i've got videos on this if you're  curious  now what they then say is to investigate  what happens  when you plot this on the same graph as  the other  equation that we got simultaneously x  squared minus y squared equals a that's  what happens when you  compare the real components on the left  and right hand side the other equation  was 2 x y is equal to  b and just like a b is something which  can change in value but it's a constant  of some kind so  i'm going to put in the b now i'm not  hesitating on this one because  this is a shape you are much more  familiar with this is just the regular  hyperbola right  um the one that you're used to dealing  with which has kind of this horizontal  vertical thing  by the way this is not just any  hyperbola this is called the rectangular  hyperbola because the two asymptotes  which in this case are the x and y axes  the the two asymptotes are at right  angles and that's actually what  rectangular means  rect just means right and angle means  angle so rectangular means right angled  that's why rectangles are called  rectangles all the angles are 90 degrees  so this is the rectangular hyperbola  that you're used to dealing with like y  equals 1 over x  but this has you know this is not just 1  over x this might be something else  over x because of the b and t